.TH MP3RGAIN 1 "October 8, 2026" "mp3rgain 3.9.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mp3rgain \- lossless MP3/AAC volume adjustment (modern mp3gain replacement)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mp3rgain
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fIFILE\fR...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mp3rgain
adjusts MP3 and AAC volume without re-encoding by modifying the
.I global_gain
field in each frame.
This preserves audio quality while achieving permanent volume changes.
.PP
Changes are lossless and reversible. For MP3, undo data is stored in APEv2 tags.
For AAC in an MP4/M4A container, it is stored in iTunes freeform metadata tags.
For a raw ADTS
.I .aac
stream, which has no container to hold them, it is stored in an ID3v2 tag.
.PP
.B mp3rgain
is a replacement for the classic
.BR mp3gain (1)
tool, written in Rust. It accepts mp3gain's options and adds lossless AAC gain
adjustment, BS.1770 loudness analysis and recursive directory processing.
See
.B COMPATIBILITY
for the few behaviours that differ.
.PP
Without an action option,
.B mp3rgain
analyzes each file and prints the recommended track and album gain without
modifying anything.
When several actions are given, only one runs, in this order of precedence:
.BR \-x ,
.BR "\-s d" ,
.BR "\-s c" ,
.BR \-u ,
.B \-a
(unless
.BR \-e ),
.B \-r
or
.BR \-e ,
.BR \-l ,
.BR \-g .
For example,
.B \-r\ \-g\ 2
applies track gain and ignores
.BR \-g .
.PP
Each file is processed once, however many times it is named: listed twice,
through a symbolic link alongside its target, or found under overlapping
.B \-R
directories. The first name given is the one reported, and a note on standard
error says how many were skipped.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS "Gain Options"
.TP
.BI \-g " i"
Apply gain of
.I i
steps without analysis. Each step equals 1.5 dB (fixed by the MP3
specification).
Positive values increase volume, negative values decrease it.
Existing
.B REPLAYGAIN_*
tags are left unchanged, so they no longer describe the audio afterwards.
.TP
.BI \-d " n"
Modify the suggested gain by
.I n
dB. With
.B \-r
or
.B \-a
it shifts the target relative to the reference level (89 dB, or the
.B \-\-rg2
/
.B \-\-r128
target), rounded to whole 1.5 dB steps; with
.B \-\-tags\-only
it is applied exactly. Without an action it shifts the recommendation the
analysis prints.
.TP
.BI \-l " channel gain"
Apply gain to one channel only.
.I channel
is 0 for left or 1 for right.
.I gain
is the number of steps. MP3 only; on AAC files this is an error.
.TP
.BI \-m " i"
Modify the suggested gain by integer
.I i
steps, in addition to any
.BR \-d .
Used with
.B \-r
or
.BR \-a .
.SS "ReplayGain Analysis"
.TP
.B \-r
Analyze and apply track gain. Each file is normalized individually to the
reference level, 89 dB with the default ReplayGain 1.0 analysis.
.TP
.B \-a
Analyze and apply album gain.
All files given on the command line are treated as one album and
normalized together, which is the same rule as
.BR mp3gain (1)'s
.BR \-a .
Use
.B \-\-album\-by
or
.B \-\-album\-depth
to split one invocation into several albums instead.
.TP
.BI \-\-album\-by " how"
With
.BR \-a ,
choose what counts as one album instead of pooling every file into one, so
a whole library can be album\-tagged in a single run. Cannot be combined with
.BR \-e .
.RS
.TP
.B dir
One album per parent directory.
.B \-\-per\-directory
is an alias for this. Works on untagged libraries, but a release whose discs
live in subdirectories is scanned as one album per disc.
.TP
.B tag
One album per release, keyed on MUSICBRAINZ_ALBUMID when present and
otherwise on ALBUMARTIST (or ARTIST) plus ALBUM. Handles discs in
subdirectories and a tree of mixed depth in one pass. Files
carrying no ALBUM tag fall back to directory grouping and are reported
rather than pooled together. When two releases share one artist and album
string, a repeated track number inside the group is reported with the
directories involved.
.RE
.IP
An album that fails analysis is reported and the run continues with the next
one. In JSON output the results appear in an
.B albums
array, each entry carrying either
.B directory
or
.BR album_artist / album_title
depending on how the group was formed. Albums are analyzed concurrently, so
the worker threads stay busy across album boundaries. The JSON report and the
exit status stay deterministically ordered, exactly as under
.BR "\-j 1" .
Text and TSV output is written as each album finishes, so its group order
follows completion rather than the order the albums were given in. Each
album's block is still emitted whole and its rows stay in file order. Use
.B \-j 1
when the output has to be byte-reproducible between runs.
.IP
In
.B dir
mode, sibling directories that share an ALBUM tag and look like discs of one
release are reported, since that grouping writes a separate album gain per
disc. Two different editions of the same album are left alone: they are
already grouped correctly by directory.
.TP
.BI \-\-album\-depth " n"
With
.B \-a
and
.BR \-R ,
one album per directory
.I n
levels below each directory argument. This is the grouping unit for a library
that is not tagged: it needs no shell expansion, has no command-line length
limit, and behaves the same on Windows, where
.B */*
is not expanded into arguments at all.
.B \-\-album\-depth 0
makes each directory argument one album,
.B 1
groups one level below it, and
.B 2
suits an
.I Artist/Album
tree. A file shallower than
.I n
levels groups at the deepest directory it has. Descending deeper than
.I n
is what keeps a multi\-disc release together: at
.B \-\-album\-depth 2
over
.IR Artist/Album ,
.I disc1
and
.I disc2
both group at
.IR Artist/Album .
When directory arguments overlap, the deepest one owns the file.
Mutually exclusive with
.B \-\-album\-by
and
.BR \-\-per\-directory .
.TP
.B \-\-rg2
Measure loudness with the ReplayGain 2.0 algorithm (ITU-R BS.1770
integrated loudness, gated) against the \-18 LUFS reference instead of
the default ReplayGain 1.0 algorithm. The reference corresponds to the
same perceived level as the classic 89 dB SPL reference; only the
measurement is different. The gain applied to the audio is still rounded to
whole 1.5 dB steps. Combines with
.BR \-r ,
.BR \-a ,
.BR \-e ,
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-m ,
.BR \-k ,
.B \-\-true\-peak
and
.BR \-\-tags\-only .
Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-\-r128 .
.TP
.B \-\-r128
Like
.BR \-\-rg2 ,
but normalizes to the EBU R128 broadcast target of \-23 LUFS.
Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-\-rg2 .
.TP
.B \-\-true\-peak
Measure the true peak (ITU-R BS.1770-4 Annex 2) for the
\fBREPLAYGAIN_*_PEAK\fR tags instead of the sample peak. The decoded signal is
oversampled 4x below 88.2 kHz and 2x at or above it, so inter-sample peaks are
caught and the value can exceed 1.0.
Requires
.B \-\-rg2
or
.BR \-\-r128 ;
the default ReplayGain 1.0 analysis keeps mp3gain's sample peak.
.TP
.B \-\-tags\-only
Write the analysis to \fBREPLAYGAIN_*\fR tags and leave the audio untouched, the way
.BR loudgain (1)
and
.BR rsgain (1)
work. The frame-level
.I global_gain
fields are not modified, so a listener can still turn ReplayGain off in their player.
The tag holds the full gain rather than a residual, and no
\fBMP3GAIN_UNDO\fR, \fBMP3GAIN_MINMAX\fR or \fBMP3GAIN_ALBUM_MINMAX\fR is written since
there is no gain change to undo or describe; an existing undo tag is kept.
Requires
.BR \-r ,
.B \-a
or
.BR \-e ,
and cannot be combined with
.BR \-g ,
.BR \-l ,
.BR \-u ,
.BR \-w ,
.B \-x
or
.BR "\-s c" / "\-s d" / "\-s s" .
.B \-d
and
.B \-m
shift the written value instead of the applied gain, and apply exactly (no 1.5 dB
step rounding, since a tag holds a float).
.B \-k
caps the written value at the file's headroom so a player applying the tag cannot clip.
.TP
.B \-e
Skip album analysis even when processing multiple files.
On its own, or with
.BR \-a ,
it analyzes and applies track gain like
.BR \-r .
(In
.BR mp3gain (1),
.B \-e
without
.B \-r
or
.B \-a
only analyzes.)
.TP
.B \-\-skip\-errors
Continue album analysis
.RB ( \-a )
past files that fail to read instead of aborting. Failed files are
reported and excluded from the album gain calculation.
.TP
.BI \-i " n"
Which audio track of a multi-track MP4 file to analyze (default: 0).
The gain itself is always applied to the first audio track, with a warning
when the file has more than one.
.SS "Undo and Tag Operations"
.TP
.B \-u
Undo gain changes using the stored undo tag (APEv2 or ID3v2 for MP3, an
iTunes freeform atom for AAC in MP4, ID3v2 for raw ADTS). The
.B REPLAYGAIN_*
values are removed as well, since they described the adjusted audio.
Every audio frame is restored exactly, except frames whose
.I global_gain
was clamped at 0 or 255. The file as a whole is byte-identical to the original
only when no ID3v2 tag was written: with the default MP3 layout, an existing
ID3v2 tag stays rewritten as ID3v2.4 and a file that had none keeps an empty
ID3v2 header. A file that was never adjusted is an error.
.TP
.BI \-s " mode"
Stored tag handling mode:
.RS
.TP
.B c
Check/show stored tag information.
.TP
.B d
Delete stored tag information, the undo tag included. Combined with \fB\-u\fR, the frame-level gain is undone first, then the remaining tags are deleted; deleting alone would destroy the undo information.
.TP
.B s
Skip stored tags: write no undo or ReplayGain tags, so an MP3 change cannot be undone. For AAC files under \fB\-r\fR/\fB\-a\fR this mode is ignored and the tags are written.
.TP
.B r
Force recalculation, ignoring stored tags (the default; kept for mp3gain compatibility). Overrides \fB\-s R\fR.
.TP
.B R
Reuse stored ReplayGain tags with \fB\-r\fR/\fB\-a\fR instead of re-analyzing, rescanning only files whose tags are missing. This is mp3gain's default behavior. In album mode, one file with missing album tags, or album tags that disagree, rescans the whole album. Ignored with \fB\-s r\fR, \fB\-d\fR/\fB\-m\fR, \fB\-\-rg2\fR/\fB\-\-r128\fR, or when a stored tag came from a BS.1770 analysis.
.TP
.B i
Put every MP3 tag in ID3v2 TXXX frames, \fBMP3GAIN_UNDO\fR and \fBMP3GAIN_MINMAX\fR
included (\fBMP3GAIN_ALBUM_MINMAX\fR exists only in APEv2, so it is not written).
Without \fBi\fR or \fBa\fR,
MP3 tags are split since 3.2.0: \fBREPLAYGAIN_*\fR in ID3v2, \fBMP3GAIN_*\fR in APEv2.
.TP
.B a
Put every MP3 tag in APEv2, which is mp3gain's layout and was the default before 3.2.0.
.RE
.IP
.B i
and
.B a
affect MP3 only: AAC in MP4 always uses freeform atoms and raw ADTS always
uses ID3v2.
If both are given, the last one wins.
.SS "Analysis Options"
.TP
.B \-x
Only find and display the maximum amplitude of the file.
Does not apply any changes.
.SS "Behavior Options"
.TP
.B \-p
Preserve the original file modification time after writing.
.TP
.B \-c
Do not print clipping warnings.
.TP
.B \-k
Prevent clipping by automatically limiting gain. With
.BR \-r ,
.B \-a
or
.B \-e
the limit comes from the decoded peak and is rounded down to whole steps, so a
file that already clips can end up attenuated. With
.B \-g
it only stops any frame's
.I global_gain
from exceeding 255; the decoded peak is not measured.
Without
.BR \-k ,
a gain that would clip is applied anyway and a warning is printed;
.B mp3rgain
never prompts.
.TP
.B \-w
Wrap gain values around instead of clamping when they exceed the
valid range (0\-255). Disables the clipping check.
.TP
.B \-t
Use a temporary file for writing. Accepted for compatibility and has no effect:
since 2.9.6 every write goes to a temporary file that replaces the original.
.TP
.B \-f
Assume MPEG 2 Layer III. Accepted for compatibility but has no effect.
.TP
.B \-q
Quiet mode. Suppress most output.
.TP
.B \-R
Process directories recursively, collecting files with the extensions
.IR .mp3 ,
.IR .m4a ,
.I .aac
and
.I .mp4
(case-insensitive). Symbolic links to directories are not followed. Without
.BR \-R ,
a directory argument is not expanded.
.TP
\fB\-j\fR \fIn\fR, \fB\-\-threads\fR \fIn\fR
Number of worker threads for the per-file work: analysis, gain application,
undo and tag writes. Default is the number of
available CPUs; 0 also means auto, and 1 forces the legacy serial path.
See also the
.B MP3RGAIN_THREADS
environment variable.
.IP
With more than one thread, the
.B text
and
.B tsv
output is written as each unit finishes, so a program reading the output can
start parsing before the run ends. Rows therefore appear in completion order
rather than in the order the files were given. A unit is never split or
interleaved with another, and
.B json
is unaffected: its records stay in input order at every thread count.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Dry-run mode. Show what would be done without making changes.
.TP
.BI \-o " format"
Output format:
.BR text " (default),"
.BR json ", or"
.BR tsv " (tab-separated values; " db " is an alias)."
.B \-o
not followed by one of these words selects
.BR tsv ,
as in mp3gain.
.B tsv
prints the mp3gain-compatible header and one row per file, with the
.B File
column holding the path as given on the command line. The rows are also
emitted while applying gain
.RB ( \-r ", " \-a ", " \-e ", " \-g ", " \-l ).
The
.B Max Amplitude
column uses mp3gain's 16-bit sample scale (peak multiplied by 32768) in the
default ReplayGain 1.0 mode, and the ReplayGain float peak (the value written
to REPLAYGAIN_*_PEAK) under
.BR \-\-rg2 " and " \-\-r128 .
Without an action,
.B json
reports frame statistics only (frame count, MPEG version, channel mode and the
.I global_gain
range), not the loudness analysis.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Show version information and exit. (\fB\-v\fR is not verbose mode.)
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help message and exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B mp3rgain song.mp3
Show the recommended gain, peak and
.I global_gain
range without modifying the file.
.TP
.B mp3rgain \-g 2 song.mp3
Apply +2 steps (+3.0 dB).
.TP
.B mp3rgain \-g \-3 song.mp3
Apply \-3 steps (\-4.5 dB).
.TP
.B mp3rgain \-r song.mp3
Analyze and apply track gain (ReplayGain).
.TP
.B mp3rgain \-a *.mp3
Analyze and apply album gain, treating all the files as one album.
.TP
.B mp3rgain \-a \-\-album\-by=tag \-R /music
Album gain for a whole library, one album per release.
.TP
.B mp3rgain \-a \-\-tags\-only *.mp3
Write album and track ReplayGain tags without modifying the audio.
.TP
.B mp3rgain \-u song.mp3
Undo previous gain changes.
.TP
.B mp3rgain \-k \-g 5 song.mp3
Apply gain, limited so that no frame's global_gain exceeds 255.
.TP
.B mp3rgain \-n \-r *.mp3
Preview track gain changes without modifying files.
.TP
.B mp3rgain \-r \-n \-o json song.mp3
Report the track gain that would be applied, in JSON.
.TP
.B mp3rgain \-R /path/to/music
Analyze all audio files in a directory tree.
.SH GAIN VALUES
Each gain step corresponds to 1.5 dB (20\(mulog10(2)/4 = 1.5051 dB), which is
fixed by the MP3 specification.
The
.I global_gain
field has a valid range of 0\-255, limiting the effective gain adjustment.
.PP
Common gain values:
.RS
.nf
Steps  dB
   1    +1.5
   2    +3.0
   3    +4.5
  \-1    \-1.5
  \-2    \-3.0
.fi
.RE
.SH REPLAYGAIN
By default
.B mp3rgain
implements the original ReplayGain 1.0 algorithm:
.IP \(bu 2
Equal-loudness filter (Yule-Walker + Butterworth high-pass)
.IP \(bu 2
RMS calculation in 50ms windows
.IP \(bu 2
95th percentile statistical analysis
.IP \(bu 2
89 dB reference level
.PP
This gives the same values as the original mp3gain. Tools based on ITU-R
BS.1770 (rsgain, loudgain, foobar2000's scanner, ffmpeg's loudnorm filter)
measure loudness differently, so their values differ unless
.B \-\-rg2
or
.B \-\-r128
is used.
.PP
On AAC,
.B mp3rgain
may report a higher peak than
.BR rsgain (1)
or foobar2000 while the gain values agree. Lossy AAC decodes to samples that can
exceed full scale, and decoders disagree about whether to clamp them:
.B mp3rgain
reports the decoded signal as it is, which is the headroom a float playback path
actually needs. See the peak section of
.I COMPARISON.md
for the measurements.
.SH SUPPORTED FORMATS
.TP
.B MP3
MPEG-1, MPEG-2 and MPEG-2.5 Layer III: reading, analyzing, and modifying gain.
.TP
.B AAC/M4A/MP4
Lossless bitstream gain adjustment and undo, including the audio track of a
video MP4. For HE-AAC only the AAC core is adjusted and the SBR data is left as
it is; this case is untested.
.TP
.B AAC (raw ADTS, .aac)
Lossless bitstream gain adjustment and undo, as for AAC in MP4. Undo and
.B REPLAYGAIN_*
go into an ID3v2 tag, since a raw stream has no container for them.
.TP
.B ALAC, DRM-protected M4P
Recognized but not adjustable. Reported as skipped, without affecting the exit
status of a batch.
.TP
.B FLAC, Ogg, Opus, WAV
Not supported. Named on the command line they fail with an error;
.B \-R
ignores them.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MP3RGAIN_THREADS
Number of worker threads, equivalent to
.BR \-j .
The explicit
.B \-j
flag takes precedence. A value of 0 means auto (number of available CPUs).
.SH FILES
For MP3 the
.B REPLAYGAIN_*
values are stored in ID3v2
.B TXXX
frames and the
.B MP3GAIN_*
values in an APEv2 tag, which is where each family's readers look; see
.BR \-s " a and " \-s " i to put both in one container."
For AAC in MP4 both families go into iTunes freeform atoms (with lowercase
names, such as
.BR mp3rgain_undo ),
and for raw ADTS into ID3v2. The values written are:
.TP
.B REPLAYGAIN_TRACK_GAIN
Track gain in dB. After gain is applied, the residual relative to the
adjusted audio.
.TP
.B REPLAYGAIN_TRACK_PEAK
Track peak, linear, where 1.0 is full scale. Can exceed 1.0.
.TP
.B REPLAYGAIN_ALBUM_GAIN
Album gain in dB (with
.BR \-a ).
.TP
.B REPLAYGAIN_ALBUM_PEAK
Album peak, linear (with
.BR \-a ).
.TP
.B REPLAYGAIN_ALGORITHM
.B ITU\-R BS.1770
under
.B \-\-rg2
and
.BR \-\-r128 ;
not written by the default ReplayGain 1.0 analysis.
.TP
.B MP3GAIN_UNDO
Information required to restore original gain values.
.TP
.B MP3GAIN_MINMAX
Minimum and maximum
.I global_gain
values of the file.
.TP
.B MP3GAIN_ALBUM_MINMAX
Minimum and maximum
.I global_gain
values across the album (MP3 with
.BR \-a ,
APEv2 only).
.PP
Every write goes to a temporary file
.RI .mp3rgain_temp_ pid _ n . ext
in the same directory, which is synced to disk and then renamed over the
original, so a failure leaves the original untouched. The original's
permissions are copied to the new file. A symbolic link is written through:
the temporary file is created next to the link's target and replaces it, so
the link stays a link. Hard links are not carried over, as with any rename:
the name given gets the new file and every other hard link keeps the old
audio. Two hard-link names listed together are two files, and each ends up
with its own adjusted copy.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success. Files skipped as unsupported (ALAC, M4P) do not change this. An
unknown short option, such as
.BR \-Z ,
only prints a warning, as in mp3gain.
.TP
.B 1
Error occurred (invalid arguments, unknown long option, file not found, etc.).
A batch in which any single file failed exits 1, including the read-only
commands (plain analysis,
.BR \-s " c and " \-x ),
so a scripted scan cannot mistake an unreadable file for a clean run.
.SH SECURITY
.B mp3rgain
is written in Rust, and its source contains no
.B unsafe
blocks. It does not contain the C code behind the published mp3gain and
aacgain CVEs (mpglib, mpglibDBL,
.IR apetag.c ,
faad2, mp4v2), so those bugs do not carry over.
.SH COMPATIBILITY
.B mp3rgain
is designed as a drop-in replacement for the original
.BR mp3gain (1)
and works with existing scripts and integrations (e.g., the beets replaygain
plugin). The audio frames it writes for
.B \-g
are byte-identical to mp3gain's, including the CRCs of CRC-protected frames.
Differences to be aware of:
.IP \(bu 2
Stored analysis is re-computed by default; use
.B \-s\ R
for mp3gain's reuse behaviour.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-e
on its own applies track gain.
.IP \(bu 2
A gain that may clip is applied with a warning instead of an interactive prompt.
.IP \(bu 2
The plain analysis never writes tags.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-g
and
.B \-l
do not update existing
.B REPLAYGAIN_*
tags, and
.B \-u
removes them.
.IP \(bu 2
Since 3.2.0, MP3 ReplayGain tags go to ID3v2 instead of APEv2;
.B \-s\ a
restores mp3gain's layout.
.IP \(bu 2
A file listed more than once is processed once. mp3gain processes it once per
listing:
.B \-g\ 2
on a file listed twice moves the audio by 4 steps and records 2 in the undo
tag.
.IP \(bu 2
A symbolic link is written through to its target; mp3gain replaces the link
with a modified copy.
